ally isn't an orphan  crop anymore because of the recent  improvement work that's been conducted  on it increasing the yields and the  adaptation to various abiotic stresses  that i used to face  um  however most of these  crops have while adaptation to the  regions that they're grown in which  tends to be harsh climates with  water scarcity and also various abiotic  and biotic factors  um  also  they uh some of these crops tend to have  pretty good nutritional benefits um in  terms of having some  nutrients that aren't found in  major crops for example tess  is a cereal crop that's cultivated in  ethiopia and i'll talk about it later on  but it is a cereal grain  with high calcium and iron and it's also  gluten free so it's actually gaining  popularity now in the western the  functional or gluten free food market  because that's this characteristic  um however though even though these  crops have all these benefits both in an  ergonomic and nutritional sense  they tend to be very low protein  productivity and labor intensive um for  cultivation and also post-harvest  processing  and this is based on  mainly because of the fact that they  haven't had much improvement other than  the selection process that farmers do  when planting these crops  and so um  hopefully with the advent of gene  editing tools we can apply  these techniques to improve these crops  and when we talk about gene editing  tools i want to make sure that everyone  is on the same page so generally allows  for a precise targeted modification of  genomes  we're talking about plant genomes here  however it does require um genomic  resource data or reference genome and  the knowledge of of  genes and their functions which um  traditional greeting doesn't really  require right  but it is more more rapid and more  precise if you have those um  those data on this those resources  there are various tools available  currently for  specific applications such as insertions  and deletions using talents or crisper  cast mines and then base editors are  the most recently reported  which allow for specific modifications  of base pairs or amino acids for a more  precise and  alteration of the gene expression rather  than  deletion or insertion  so there have been various applications  of these gene editing tools and various  for our major crops shown here and the  application range from  benefits such as uh resistant to abiotic  stress factors biotic stress factors  overall  yield um as well as consumer benefits so  i was involved in a project  that improved potato storability  by just targeting one gene using  talons and so we were able to  knock out  this gene that controls for um that  controls the reduction of sugars  which um when they are stored and  processed at high temperature for  example when making um  fries or chips  these reduced sugar tend to  turn into these bitter brown spots  that um have been reported to have  acrylamides which is a possible  carcinogen so by just knocking out this  one gene we're able to produce potato  lines that are healthier to consumers  so moving these gene editing approaches  to organ crops require various  advancements in technology such as  sequencing phenotyping and  bioinformatics and there has been rapid  improvements in these  technologies  reference genomes for orphan crops and  four different varieties have been  are  reported and are being  put into data public accessible  databases which will help researchers  globally to be able to access these and  perform their own gene editing  applications in  the class that they're interested in and  also these advancements are helping  improve  the gene identification and functional  characterization work and so it takes  less time than it used to take with some  of um with the utilization of these  tools  another advancement that's key is  advancements in plant transformation  methods you might have heard that  plant transformation tends to be the  bottleneck for  uh gene editing tissue culture  specifically  delivery of the  editing reagents and then into the plant  tissue and then going through tissue  culture to try to get a region to try to  regenerate a transformed  plants usually takes a lot of time  expertise and resources  that most  labs in  especially developing countries don't  have the capacity for so genotype  independent transformation methods using  developmental regulators  which has been really  at the forefront of this  field  is  helping move forward  diminish this bottleneck and corsava  agrosciences has done a lot of work in  this um  in this  forefront and also  sharing their techniques and  technologies with institutions and  researchers  globally another one is the tissue calls  for free transformation methods  our lab at the damn voices lab reported  two papers last year where one used  virus  to  transform or edit uh  plants  and then another one use agrobacterium  to also edit  various different species  and this both of these methods gives the  chance the tissue culture um  process  which not only saved a lot of time but  resources as well and so some of these  advancements are key of course  communication and collaborative  platforms are also um have grown through  this covet  time  zoom and other platforms allow for  data sharing and  working together uh for training  purposes  so this allows for  the um sharing of  these tools and technique with  researchers that  may not have had access to it in  previous years  there are various um traits  improvements that can be carried out in  orphaned crops as you can see here some  of these orphan crops  suffer from  these um  biotic and abiotic factors and there are  various genes that have already been  characterized to improve some of the  tree simple plant architecture for  example  most of the cereal orchard crops tend to  suffer from plant lodging which was a  huge issue for before the green  revolution and both  wheat and  rice and these genes sd1 and rhq1 were  introduced through the  traditional breeding that took place  during the  green revolution and these could easily  be introduced in  uh orphan cross or from cereal crops  through gene editing as well as some of  these  other  traits and genes associated with them  and so these are  very low-hanging fruits that can easily  be introduced in  various orphan crops  in order to reach their yield potential  and improve their  nutrition as well  one  case study i want to talk about is  cassava cassava is the most important  food crop  globally and also it feeds one third of  africa's population  um it is well adapted to that  environment  but unfortunately it does  require it's a labor-intensive  cultivation process and post-harvest  processing  and  the first reference genome was uh  reported in 2009 and since then  2017 the first demonstration of crispr  castline was reported and since then  there have been a lot of  ongoing work  in improving  cassava through  gene editing approaches  one  from our lab in collaboration with the  danforth institute was  developing herbicides following cassava  targeting the esps the epsps gene using  crispr cas9 and  this glyphosate tolerant cassava is now  being  tested filled  field testing  in the country um in something in africa  that's culturally cassava  another example that dear to my heart is  tough which is a cereal crop that is the  staple um  crop in ethiopia i grew up eating it it  covers about 30 percent of  the cereal the  land that is allocated to cereal  production in the country um however  when  you look at the yield it's the lowest  the lowest yielding of all the cereals  that are also cultivated in the country  and this has to do with the various  limitations listed here susceptibility  to wheat  plant lodging and seed quality  the improvement of  tests is now just starting and this was  because of the fact that we didn't  really have um  a full complete genome of tests until  recently last year a complete genome was  reported and made available and since  then there's an ongoing work to try to  introduce some of the genes that i  talked about previously to  reduce some of these limitations and  reach the yolk potential  of self  so with that i would like to conclude  stating that  technology and advancements and  increased access to these  technologies are key to  using  advanced plant biotechnology tools for  orphan crops and so i talked about  availability of genome being  key and also capacity building in  developing countries is another key  factor in moving  this technology forward so instead of  conducting the research of improving  these crops  in western countries  it's best if we can build a capacity  train scientists researchers in  developing countries so that they are  able to carry out these uh improvement  work on their own staple crops and  hopefully acceptance of these um  edited products  and better policy landscapes and these  regions can really  bring about  these  advancements to farmers and consumers  finally  i am hopeful that we will be able to  adapt to climate change using these  advanced  tools  by de novo domestication of wild type  crops which has already been reported  and also improving these various  orphan crops and different varieties to  be able to develop climate resilient  agricultural  landscapes  and  that is also high yielding and that  meets the nutritional needs of the  growing population  so with that i would like to thank you  for your intent attention and i would  i look forward to our discussions later  on
 

